(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_botany_principlesofinheritanceandvariation=[{id:"neet-2026-q113",pn:113,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Sickle-cell anaemia",q:"Which one of the following disorders is caused by the substitution of Glutamic acid (Glu) by Valine (Val) at the sixth position of the beta globin chain of the haemoglobin molecule?",o:["Haemophilia","Thalassemia","Sickle-cell anaemia","Phenylketonuria"],c:2,s:`Sickle-cell anaemia is an autosome-linked recessive trait.
It passes from parents to offspring when both partners are carriers of the gene.
The defect is caused by substitution of Glu by Val at the sixth position of the beta globin chain of haemoglobin.`},{id:"neet-2026-q114",pn:114,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Inheritance patterns",q:`Match List I with List II:
A. Incomplete dominance \u2014 I. Human skin colour
B. Co-dominance \u2014 II. Inheritance of flower colour in Antirrhinum sp.
C. Pleiotropy \u2014 III. Phenylketonuria disease in humans
D. Polygenic inheritance \u2014 IV. ABO blood groups
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],c:0,s:`Flower colour in snapdragon (Antirrhinum sp.) is an example of incomplete dominance, so A-II.
ABO blood groups show co-dominance in individuals of AB group (I\u1D2CI\u1D2E), so B-IV.
Phenylketonuria is pleiotropy, since the gene responsible leads to multiple phenotypic effects, so C-III.
Human skin colour is controlled by three pairs of non-allelic genes, so it is polygenic inheritance, so D-I.`},{id:"neet-2026-q118",pn:118,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Sex determination in honeybees",q:`Which of the following statements are true with reference to the sex-determination in honeybees?
A. An offspring formed from the union of a sperm and an egg, develops as a female (queen or worker).
B. An unfertilized egg develops as a male by parthenogenesis.
C. A male has half the number of chromosomes than that of a female.
D. Males produce sperms by meiosis.
E. Honeybees have a haplodiploid sex-determination system.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["B, C, D and E only","A, B, C and D only","A, B, D and E only","A, B, C and E only"],c:3,s:`In honeybees, males have half the number of chromosomes of females.
Females are diploid with 32 chromosomes; males are haploid with 16 chromosomes.
This is the haplodiploid sex-determination system.
A special feature is that males produce sperms by mitosis, not meiosis, so D is false.
True statements: A, B, C and E.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q142",pn:142,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Mendel's monohybrid cross",q:`Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.
Assertion A : In an experiment, Mendel observed that the F1 progeny plants are all tall and none are dwarf.
Reason R : Stem height is a contrasting trait, with tall being dominant and dwarf being recessive.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :`,o:["Both A and R are correct but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is correct but R is not correct","A is not correct but R is correct","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A"],c:3,s:`Mendel crossed tall and dwarf plants and found all F\u2081 plants tall.
The tall trait (T) is dominant over the dwarf trait (t).
Because of this dominance, all F\u2081 plants are tall.`},{id:"neet-2025-q130",pn:130,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Polygenic inheritance",q:"What is the pattern of inheritance for polygenic trait?",o:["Mendelian inheritance pattern","Non-mendelian inheritance pattern","Autosomal dominant pattern","X-linked recessive inheritance pattern"],c:1,s:`Polygenic inheritance means a trait is controlled by two or more genes.
Disorders caused by mutation in a single gene pass on as per Mendelian principle.
A polygenic trait shows a non-Mendelian inheritance pattern.`},{id:"neet-2024-q118",pn:118,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Test cross",q:"In a plant, black seed color (BB/Bb) is dominant over white seed color (bb). In order to find out the genotype of the black seed plant, with which of the following genotype will you cross it?",o:["Bb","BB/Bb","BB","bb"],c:3,s:`To find the genotype of a black-seeded plant, it is crossed with the white-seeded (homozygous recessive) plant.
This cross is called a test cross.
So the black plant (BB/Bb) must be crossed with bb.`},{id:"neet-2024-q119",pn:119,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Incomplete dominance",q:"A pink flowered Snapdragon plant was crossed with a red flowered snapdragon plant. What type of phenotype/s is/are expected in the progeny?",o:["Only pink flowered plants","Red, pink as well as white flowered plants","Only red flowered plants","Red flowered as well as pink flowered plants"],c:3,s:`Pink snapdragon has genotype Rr.
Red snapdragon has genotype RR.
Cross Rr x RR gives RR, RR, Rr, Rr.
Phenotype ratio Red : Pink : White = 2 : 2 : 0.
So only red and pink flowered plants are obtained.`,sfig:{w:380,h:140,alt:"Punnett square of Rr crossed with RR: top R, R; left R, r; cells RR, RR, Rr, Rr; phenotype Red : Pink : White = 2 : 2 : 0.",d:[["r",2,8,234,126],["l",2,134,236,134],["l",2,126,2,134],["l",236,8,236,134],["l",2,8,82,78],["l",82,8,82,134],["l",142,8,142,134],["l",2,78,236,78],["l",2,106,236,106],["t",34,30,"\u2642"],["t",24,66,"\u2640"],["t",92,30,"R"],["t",152,30,"R"],["t",8,96,"R"],["t",92,96,"RR"],["t",152,96,"RR"],["t",8,124,"r"],["t",92,124,"Rr"],["t",152,124,"Rr"],["t",268,28,"Phenotype"],["t",268,96,"Red : Pink : White"],["t",268,122,"2"],["t",296,122,"2"],["t",330,122,"0"]]}},{id:"neet-2024-q120",pn:120,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Genetic terms",q:`Mathch List I with List II
List I:
(A) Two or more alternative forms of a gene
(B) Cross of F\u2081 progeny with homozygous recessive parent
(C) Cross of F\u2081 progeny with any of the parents
(D) Number of chromosome sets in plant
List II:
(I) Back cross
(II) Ploidy
(III) Allele
(IV) Test cross
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV"],c:0,s:`A. Alternative forms of a gene are alleles.
B. F\u2081 crossed with the homozygous recessive parent is a test cross.
C. F\u2081 crossed with either parent is a back cross.
D. The number of chromosome sets in a plant is its ploidy.
So: A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II.`},{id:"neet-2024-q130",pn:130,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Law of dominance",q:`Which one of the following can be explained on the basis of Mendel's Law of Dominance?
(A) Out of one pair of factors one is dominant and the other is recessive.
(B) Alleles do not show any expression and both the characters appear as such in F\u2082 generation
(C) Factors occur in pairs in normal diploid plants
(D) The discrete unit controlling a particular character is called factor
(E) The expression of only one of the parental characters is found in a monohybrid cross.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["B, C and D only","A, B, C, D and E","A, B and C only","A, C, D and E only"],c:3,s:`According to the Law of Dominance:
(1) Characters are controlled by discrete units called factors.
(2) Factors occur in pairs.
(3) In a dissimilar pair, one factor (dominant) masks the other (recessive).
This law explains why only one parental character is seen in a monohybrid cross.
Statement B (alleles show no expression and both characters reappear in F\u2082) is explained by the Law of Segregation, not dominance.
So A, C, D and E are correct.`},{id:"neet-2023-q110",pn:110,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Pleiotropy",q:"The phenomenon of pleiotropism refers to -",o:["More than two genes affecting a single character","Presence of several alleles of a single gene controlling a single crossover","Presence of two alleles, each of the two genes controlling a single trait","A single gene affecting multiple phenotypic expression"],c:3,s:`When one gene affects several phenotypic traits, it is a pleiotropic gene.
This phenomenon is called pleiotropism.`},{id:"neet-2023-q128",pn:128,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Linkage and gene mapping",q:"Frequency of recombination between gene pairs on same chromosome as a measure of the distance between genes to map their position on chromosome, was used for the first time by",o:["Henking","Thomas Hunt Morgan","Sutton and Boveri","Alfred Sturtevant"],c:3,s:`Alfred Sturtevant used the recombination frequency between gene pairs on one chromosome as a measure of distance between them and so mapped their positions.
Sutton and Boveri gave the chromosomal theory of inheritance.
Henking discovered the X-chromosome.
Morgan proved the chromosomal theory and proposed linkage.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q101",pn:101,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Mendelian disorders",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I : Sickle cell anaemia and Haemophilia are autosomal dominant traits.
Statement II: Sickle cell anaemia and Haemophilia are disorders of the blood.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect"],c:0,s:`Sickle cell anaemia is an autosomal recessive trait.
Haemophilia is an X-linked recessive trait.
So Statement I is wrong.
Both diseases affect the blood, so Statement II is right.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q115",pn:115,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Chromosomal theory of inheritance",q:"The chromosomal theory of inheritance was proposed by",o:["Robert Brown","Thomas Morgan","Sutton and Boveri","Gregor Mendel"],c:2,s:"Sutton and Boveri proposed the chromosomal theory of inheritance in 1902-1903."},{id:"neet-2022-q116",pn:116,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Mendel's experiments",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I : Mendel studied seven pairs of contrasting traits in pea plants and proposed the Laws of Inheritance.
Statement II : Seven characters examined by Mendel in his experiment on pea plants were seed shape and colour, flower colour, pod shape and colour, flower position and stem height.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],c:3,s:`Mendel used 14 true-breeding varieties, which make seven contrasting pairs.
The traits: seed shape, seed colour, flower colour, pod shape, pod colour, flower position, stem height.
Both statements are correct.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q139",pn:139,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Dihybrid cross ratio",q:"What is the expected percentage of F2 progeny with yellow and inflated pod in dihybrid cross experiment involving pea plants with green coloured, inflated pod and yellow coloured constricted pod?",o:["9%","100%","56.25%","18.75%"],c:3,s:`The cross is green inflated \xD7 yellow constricted.
F1 is all green inflated.
F2 ratio is 9 green inflated : 3 green constricted : 3 yellow inflated : 1 yellow constricted.
Yellow inflated is 3/16 = 18.75%.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q141",pn:141,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Down syndrome",q:"If a female individual is with small round head, furrowed tongue, partially open mouth and broad palm with characteristic palm crease. Also the physical, psychomotor and mental development is retarded. The karyotype analysis of such an individual will show",o:["Trisomy of chromosome 21","47 chromosomes with XXY sex chromosomes","45 chromosomes with XO sex chromosomes","47 Chromosomes with XYY sex chromosomes"],c:0,s:`The features describe Down syndrome.
A karyotype is the full set of metaphase chromosomes of a cell.
Down syndrome is trisomy of chromosome 21 (2n + 1), a kind of aneuploidy.
Aneuploidy results from non-disjunction of chromosomes in meiosis I or II.`},{id:"neet-2022-q143",pn:143,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Mendelian disorders",q:"Which of the following occurs due to the presence of autosome linked dominant trait?",o:["Myotonic dystrophy","Haemophilia","Thalessemia","Sickle cell anaemia"],c:0,s:`Haemophilia is X-linked recessive.
Thalassemia and sickle cell anaemia are autosomal recessive.
Myotonic dystrophy is autosomal dominant, so it is due to an autosome linked dominant trait.`},{id:"neet-2022-q149",pn:149,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Linkage and independent assortment",q:`Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).
Assertion (A) : Mendel\u2019s law of Independent assortment does not hold good for the genes that are located closely on the same chromosome.
Reason (R) : Closely located genes assort independently.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is correct but (R) is not correct","(A) is not correct but (R) is correct","Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)"],c:1,s:`Closely located genes do not assort independently, so (R) is wrong.
Independent assortment holds for genes on different chromosomes.
It fails for genes close together on one chromosome, so (A) is correct.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q161",pn:161,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Pedigree analysis symbols",q:"Select the incorrect match regarding the symbols used in Pedigree analysis",o:["Parent with male child affected with disease","Sex unspecified","Affected individual","Consanguineous mating"],c:3,s:`The symbol shown for option (4) is not the one for consanguineous mating.
Consanguineous mating means mating between relatives.`,fig:{w:360,h:240,alt:"Pedigree symbols: (1) parents with an affected male child, (2) diamond for sex unspecified, (3) filled square for an affected individual, (4) square joined to a circle by a single line.",d:[["t",4,48,"(1)"],["r",30,14,22,22],["c",86,25,11],["l",52,25,75,25],["l",64,25,64,50],["r",53,50,22,22,"F"],["t",104,48,"Parent with male child affected with disease"],["t",4,112,"(2)"],["p","M50 92L70 112L50 132L30 112Z"],["t",82,116,"Sex unspecified"],["t",4,168,"(3)"],["r",32,148,36,36,"F"],["t",76,170,"Affected individual"],["t",4,218,"(4)"],["r",32,206,22,22],["c",84,217,11],["l",54,217,73,217],["t",100,221,"Consanguineous mating"]]}},{id:"neet-2022r-q190",pn:190,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Sex-linked inheritance",q:`A normal girl, whose mother is haemophilic marries a male with no ancestral history of haemophilia. What will be the possible phenotypes of the offsprings?
(a) Haemophilic son and haemophilic daughter.
(b) Haemophilic son and carrier daughter.
(c) Normal daughter and normal son.
(d) Normal son and haemophilic daughter.
Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :`,o:["(b) and (d) only","(a) and (b) only","(b) and (c) only","(a) and (d) only"],c:2,s:`Haemophilia is X-linked recessive.
The girl got one X\u02B0 from her haemophilic mother, so she is X\u1D34X\u02B0 (carrier).
The father is X\u1D34Y (normal).
Cross gives: X\u1D34X\u1D34 normal daughter, X\u1D34X\u02B0 carrier daughter, X\u1D34Y normal son, X\u02B0Y haemophilic son.
No haemophilic daughter is possible, so (b) and (c) are true.`},{id:"neet-2022-q194",pn:194,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Linkage and gene mapping",q:"The recombination frequency between the genes a & c is 5%, b & c is 15%, b & d is 9%, a & b is 20%, c & d is 24% and a & d is 29%. What will be the sequence of these genes on a linear chromosome?",o:["d, b, a, c","a, b, c, d","a, c, b, d","a, d, b, c"],c:2,s:`1% recombination frequency = 1 centiMorgan, and the largest value marks the two end genes.
a\u2013d is 29%, so a and d are the ends.
a\u2013c is 5% so c is next to a; c\u2013b is 15% and b\u2013d is 9%.
Check: 5 + 15 + 9 = 29. So the order is a, c, b, d.`},{id:"neet-2022-q195",pn:195,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Sex-linked inheritance",q:"If a colour blind female marries a man whose mother was colour blind, what are the chances of her progeny having color blindness?",o:["50%","75%","100%","25%"],c:2,s:`Colour blindness is X-linked recessive.
The man's mother is colour blind (XcXc), so he gets Xc from her and is colour blind (XcY).
The woman is colour blind (XcXc).
All children get Xc from the mother and Xc or Y from the father: sons XcY, daughters XcXc, so all are colour blind (100%).`}]})();
